# Write Signatures
Write component level signatures and documentation for modules, functions, classes, etc.

## Inputs

- A ticket, story, use case, or feature description with acceptance criteria.
- Any prior codebase research.
- A high-level design that defines the current state, future state, decisions made, etc.
- Data definitions that will be added and or changed.
- Top-level behavior diagram.

## Instructions
- Write signatures using the syntax of the codebase (Elixir, TypeScript, etc.).
- Use `diff` when the point is what changes and the surrounding shape already exists. Match the diff shape to the topic.
- Write signatures, documentation, pre/post conditions ONLY. Do not write implementation.
- Write signatures for production code ONLY, not tests.
- Write docstrings only for public components.
- Write a simple call-stack or call-tree diagram. Use `diff` to show changes.
- Output the results using the report format below.
- Emit the report itself. Do not wrap it in a code fence: the first line of your reply is the opening `## Signatures`. The report's top heading is `##`, and every heading inside it is `###` or deeper.

## Workflow

- Read all the inputs FULLY.
- For each new component, generate signatures and documentation. The documentation should include pre/post conditions.
- For any components that change, use `diff` to show the signature changes.
- Report using the report format below.

## Report Format
```md
## Signatures

### Call Tree
<call tree>

### Signatures
<component signatures>
```
